D. Relation to classical optimization theory
Classical theories of early stopping and iterative regularization analyze gradient descent on fixed quadratic objectives, yielding the spectral filter form fˆT =g T (K)r with gT (λ) = 1−(1−ρλ) T Yao et al.(e.g.,2007);Engl et al. (e.g.,2000). We reuse this identity, but our TTT setting differs in three critical ways.
(1) The geometry K is not fixed.Under our local linearization, the prompt-space geometry is K(Pn) = ΦΦ⊤ = JwΠU J ⊤
w . Unlike classical analyses where K (or the design) is exogenous, here K varies across prompts and changes with the update mechanism ΠU (which blocks/heads/directions are adaptable). Thus, TTT behavior is inherently prompt- and mechanism-dependent.
(2) The target is query prediction, not only in-sample fitting.Beyond the prompt correction fˆT , prediction relies
on the query-point correction ˆhT (x) =k ⊤
x qT (K)r with kx = Φφ(x;P n). Hence, success depends not only on filter choice (via qT ) but also on prompt–query coupling: only eigenmodes aligned withk x affect the query (Thm.5.2).
(3) T and update directions are on-the-fly objects.In TTT, a fixed T can be Bayes-suboptimal under test-time prior shift, and T→ ∞ can overfit (Sec.6), motivating perprompt T selection (Sec.7). Moreover, no choice of T can overcome subspace mismatch: corrections lie in S(Pn) = range(K(Pn)), so update-direction design is essential.
Together, these distinctions explain why step sensitivity and update-direction choices are intrinsic to TTT, rather than secondary hyperparameter details.
